{
  "case_id": "2008-07-636",
  "document_hash": "89e94da11ee70289ef671d29465a2596e204f1417b845140c7d7fb21c87da1bf",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-07-636",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89e94da11ee70289ef671d29465a2596e204f1417b845140c7d7fb21c87da1bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89e94da11ee7-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 1:00 p.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-07-636",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89e94da11ee70289ef671d29465a2596e204f1417b845140c7d7fb21c87da1bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89e94da11ee7-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: You did in your reply brief address the plan question?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "David A. Furlow",
      "case_id": "2008-07-636",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89e94da11ee70289ef671d29465a2596e204f1417b845140c7d7fb21c87da1bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89e94da11ee7-0000-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Department of the Treasury and the Department of Labor, now in a harmonized stance, have come forth and in their amicus for the government supports our position that the Fifth Circuit erred in its interpretation of the QDRO statute, and that's the position we take."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Amicus Curiae",
      "case_id": "2008-07-636",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89e94da11ee70289ef671d29465a2596e204f1417b845140c7d7fb21c87da1bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89e94da11ee7-0001-0000",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. KRUGER: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: The Fifth Circuit decided this case on the grounds that ERISA's anti-alienation provision forbids a divorcing spouse from relinquishing an interest in his or her ex-spouse's pension plan benefits unless the waiver takes the form of a qualified domestic relations order. We agree with Petitioner that the Fifth Circuit misinterpreted both the anti-alienation provision and the QDRO exception to that provision. But we disagree with Petitioner's further submission that merely because ERISA does not forbid waivers in the divorce context or otherwise, that a plan administrator may be required as a matter of Federal common law to recognize such waivers even when those waivers conflict with the beneficiary designation the plan administrator has on file."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2008-07-636",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89e94da11ee70289ef671d29465a2596e204f1417b845140c7d7fb21c87da1bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89e94da11ee7-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Did -- did we recognize in earlier cases that beneficiary designations are plan documents?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Amicus Curiae",
      "case_id": "2008-07-636",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89e94da11ee70289ef671d29465a2596e204f1417b845140c7d7fb21c87da1bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89e94da11ee7-0001-0004",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. KRUGER: That is correct. There is a circuit split on the question as was raised in the petition for certiorari, and the Second and Sixth Circuits are the circuits that have to date agreed with the position that we are espousing here: That the plan documents control and preclude formulation of a Federal common law rule of the sort that Petitioner proposes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2008-07-636",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89e94da11ee70289ef671d29465a2596e204f1417b845140c7d7fb21c87da1bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89e94da11ee7-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. LEVY: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: In light of the Court's questions, I want to begin with the plan documents argument and first the procedural question of whether it's properly before the Court. We think that it is. It's well within the settled doctrine of an alternative ground for affirmance. It was raised below, and the Fifth Circuit has decided the issue in other cases. We don't dispute that. So we know what the Fifth Circuit thinks about this issue, and, therefore, there is no point in a remand."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2008-07-636",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89e94da11ee70289ef671d29465a2596e204f1417b845140c7d7fb21c87da1bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89e94da11ee7-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What if they had agreed to the waiver apart from -- apart from the -- from the domestic relations order? Just apart from that, they have a separate signed waiver. We'd be in the same suit that you're -- that you say we have to avoid, wouldn't we?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Levy",
      "case_id": "2008-07-636",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89e94da11ee70289ef671d29465a2596e204f1417b845140c7d7fb21c87da1bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89e94da11ee7-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I believe it must follow the beneficiary designation. Indeed, my understanding is it has a fiduciary duty and a legal requirement to follow the plan designation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2008-07-636",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89e94da11ee70289ef671d29465a2596e204f1417b845140c7d7fb21c87da1bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89e94da11ee7-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Was this waiver only part of the divorce degree? Do you agree with your friend on that point?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2008-07-636",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89e94da11ee70289ef671d29465a2596e204f1417b845140c7d7fb21c87da1bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89e94da11ee7-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Do you have anything more to say on the plan document, the plan document issue than what you've said here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Furlow",
      "case_id": "2008-07-636",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89e94da11ee70289ef671d29465a2596e204f1417b845140c7d7fb21c87da1bf",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89e94da11ee7-0003-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It is interesting, Your Honors, that they talked about the plan documents rule, but their own documentation says, their own plan says, that the only plan documents -- and I quoted it here on page 25 of our reply brief -- \"The official plan documents are the E.I. DuPont de Nemours & Company savings and investment plan and the trust agreement,\" not beneficiary designations. So they give no notice."
    },
